1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,840
You know that moment of amazed surprise when you encounter something so unexpected?

2
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,820
It feels almost magical.

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:11,980
An idea that kind of blows your mind.

4
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:16,660
At that precise moment, I fell in love with physics.

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:19,280
General activity is incredibly spectacular.

6
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,320
There's this curving of space, stretching on time.

7
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:23,980
It's better than LST.

8
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:26,420
Haven't you had these moments in your mind?

9
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:31,040
And maybe it's that little space between drifting off to sleep or waking up.

10
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,020
Or maybe you're stoned.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:33,420
You feel things like that.

12
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:35,880
These paradoxes of truth.

13
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,020
Welcome to Wonder Cabinet.

14
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:39,760
I'm Anne Strange-Hamps.

15
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:40,920
And I'm Steve Paulson.

16
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,760
We're the creators of the Peabody Award-winning public radio show, to the best of our knowledge.

17
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:49,100
And we are so excited to be launching something new.

18
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:53,460
An interview podcast designed to keep your sense of wonder alive.

19
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:58,700
So you traveled with some people who live very close to the natural world.

20
00:00:58,780 --> 00:00:59,620
And they see things.

21
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,520
They notice things that I would never notice.

22
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,300
And I mean, I was particularly struck by a mycologist.

23
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:05,820
Yes.

24
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:11,620
Who talks about not just seeing mushrooms, but hearing, feeling when they're nearby.

25
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,680
And I'm just trying to think about what that's like.

26
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:17,540
I mean, what was it like to travel with her?

27
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,220
It was wild, Steve.

28
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:20,280
It was truly wild.

29
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,460
She was looking for tiny brown psilocybe species.

30
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:25,560
The two times she found them, she found both of them.

31
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:28,120
She would say, oh, they're near now.

32
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:28,900
I can hear them.

33
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:31,000
She calls it a fuzz in the matrix.

34
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:32,900
I don't know what that means to experience.

35
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:34,820
But boy, was she right.

36
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:36,000
We would go around two corners.

37
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:39,480
She would suddenly fall to the ground as though she'd been shot.

38
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:40,380
Woo-hoo!

39
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:45,220
And there was this tiny brown mushroom in this fast brown forest floor.

40
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:50,040
We've spent more than 30 years talking with some of the world's greatest scientists and

41
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:51,060
poets and philosophers.

42
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:54,400
This time, in this new podcast, we're going deeper.

43
00:01:55,060 --> 00:02:01,140
Longer, more intimate conversations about mystery and beauty and the deep intelligence

44
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:02,080
of the earth.

45
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:06,080
So you have caterpillars eating leaves, getting fat, getting ready.

46
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:11,780
Then they create a cocoon and they basically begin to melt and their brain liquefied.

47
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:13,280
That's the craziest thing.

48
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:19,380
The butterflies have the same memories as they did when they were caterpillars, even though

49
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:20,620
their brains have been liquefied.

50
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:22,660
How is that possible?

51
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:23,300
Right?

52
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,740
For me, that's where my wonder is.

53
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:30,420
Join us as we build a new cabinet of wonders for the modern age.

54
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:35,500
The first episodes drop January 31st, wherever you get your podcasts.

